-- #414: two ⊑-incomparable overlapping impls are correctly rejected with
-- T-CONFLICTING-IMPL — this fixture pins the RANGE.  It used to be a fabricated
-- file-start caret (0:0) on every coherence conflict, which is unusable for an
-- editor/LSP consumer: the message names the heads, but nothing points at either
-- impl.  The range must now span the head tycon of the LATER-declared impl
-- (`Pair` on the `impl C (Pair a Int)` line) — which is also the head the message
-- names FIRST, so caret and wording agree.
data Pair a b = Pair a b

interface C a where
  c : a -> Int

impl C (Pair Int b) where
  c _ = 1

impl C (Pair a Int) where
  c _ = 2

main : <IO> Unit
main = println (c (Pair 1 2))

-- ⚠️ THIS NOTE IS DELIBERATELY AT THE BOTTOM.  The golden above pins the
-- T-CONFLICTING-IMPL RANGE by absolute line number, so a note added to the header
-- would shift it and conflate "the caret moved" with the change that actually
-- happened here.  (AGENTS.md's fixture-line-count trap, paid for on 2026-07-31.)
--
-- Since #1155 (F-3c) this file emits TWO diagnostics and the golden carries both.
-- The FIRST is a goal-site T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE at `c (Pair 1 2)`, because the
-- ground goal `C (Pair Int Int)` matches `impl C (Pair Int b)` and
-- `impl C (Pair a Int)`, which are ⊑-incomparable -- DICT-SEMANTICS §6 C1's
-- "ambiguous overlap", reported at the goal instead of silently taking the
-- first-declared impl.  The SECOND is the declaration-time coherence finding this
-- fixture is about.  They are different rules at different sites (declaration-time
-- condition (a) vs per-goal condition (c)); see `reportAmbiguousOverlap` in
-- compiler/types/typecheck.mdk for why the goal-site one is NOT suppressed when the
-- declaration-time one fires.
--
-- ⚠️ Since F-3d (#614/#311) the SECOND is a `W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS` WARNING
-- (severity 2, kind "warning", carrying a `help`), not `T-CONFLICTING-IMPL`.  The
-- FIXTURE'S OWN SUBJECT IS UNCHANGED: #414 is about the RANGE, and this file still
-- pins that the coherence finding spans the head tycon of the later-declared impl.
-- What moved is its code and severity.  That improves the pairing F-3c accepted as
-- an ERROR-QUALITY dim X/R cost -- but ONLY ON THIS `--json` SURFACE, which is why
-- the claim lives in this fixture: the two diagnostics no longer read as two
-- same-severity errors with the same primary remedy printed out of source order, but
-- as one error plus one advisory note.  ⚠️ A HUMAN `medaka check` on this file sees
-- neither improvement nor the warning: the CLI's error arm prints only
-- `filter isDiagError diags`, so warnings are dropped whenever any error fired.
-- Pre-existing, not caused or repaired here.
-- ⚠️ The program is still REJECTED (exit 1) -- by the goal-site error, at the
-- CLOSED goal.  Its sibling with an OPEN goal now compiles; see
-- test/dict_fixtures/s6-2-t4-open-goal-deferred.mdk and #1183.
